{"metadata":{"title":"Declaring a kind","modules":[{"name":"LinkerSets"}],"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Pick the number that tags your declarations, and decide whether your users write it."}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/linkersets\/declaringakind"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"Overview","level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A kind is a 32-bit number that every record carries. A "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/LinkerSet"},{"type":"text","text":" of a kind holds the records"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"declared with that kind and no others, so one program can hold your routes, another library’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"analytics events and a third’s migrations without any of them seeing the others."}]},{"anchor":"Choosing-the-number","level":3,"type":"heading","text":"Choosing the number"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Any number other than 0 works. 0 marks an empty slot, and the macros refuse it. The convention is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"four ASCII letters that name the thing, read as one byte each: “rout” is ","type":"text"},{"code":"r","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"o","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"u","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"t","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", which is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"0x72","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"0x6F","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"0x75","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"0x74","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", so the kind is ","type":"text"},{"code":"0x726F_7574","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". The letters make it unlikely that two","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"libraries pick the same number, and they stay readable in a hex dump of the binary. Swift computes","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"one for you:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let kind = \"rout\".utf8.reduce(0) { $0 << 8 | UInt32($1) }  \/\/ 0x726F_7574"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Give the kind a name for the code that reads it:"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["extension LinkerSetKind {","    static let routes = LinkerSetKind(rawValue: 0x726F_7574)  \/\/ \"rout\"","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A library that reads the set for its users keeps the name internal: the macro writes the kind as a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"number, so its users’ files never need it.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"When-two-libraries-pick-the-same-number","level":3,"type":"heading","text":"When two libraries pick the same number"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"No compiler sees every module at once, so nothing can check that a kind is unique. If two libraries","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"choose the same number, their records land in the same set. A record still hands over its value","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"only when asked for its own type, so a set of a type your library owns (","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Route"},{"text":", not ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"String"},{"text":")","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"holds only your declarations. Read your records as a type of your own, and a clash costs nothing","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"but the time to skip the other library’s records.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"Writing-the-kind-at-each-declaration","level":3,"type":"heading","text":"Writing the kind at each declaration"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/LinkerSetEntry(kind:)","type":"reference"},{"text":" takes the kind as an integer literal. It has to be a literal because the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"macro writes it into the binary at compile time; a named constant isn’t visible to the macro:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["enum Routes {","    @LinkerSetEntry(kind: 0x726F_7574)  \/\/ \"rout\"","    static let home = Route(path: \"\/\")","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"That suits a kind used in a few places. A typo in the number puts the declaration in the wrong"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"set without any error, though."}]},{"anchor":"Giving-your-users-a-macro","level":3,"type":"heading","text":"Giving your users a macro"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A library that asks its users to register things gives them a macro with the kind built in, so"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"they write "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Route"},{"type":"text","text":" and never see the number. The macro lives in a small compiler plugin built on"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the LinkerSetsMacroSupport module, whose documentation walks through the plugin, the macro’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"declaration and the package’s targets. The plugin can also require a protocol of every type it"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"registers, so "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Event"},{"type":"text","text":" on a type that isn’t an event fails to compile where it’s written."}]}]}],"seeAlsoSections":[{"generated":true,"anchor":"Guides","identifiers":["doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/FindingRecords","doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/WhatTheMacrosRefuse","doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/ReplacingARegistry","doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/PlatformsAndLinking"],"title":"Guides"}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"sections":[],"kind":"article","hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets"]]},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/DeclaringAKind"},"references":{"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/ReplacingARegistry":{"url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/replacingaregistry","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Move a central list of event types, routes or registrations onto declarations that find"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"themselves."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/ReplacingARegistry","kind":"article","title":"Replacing a hand-written registry","role":"article"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/LinkerSetEntry(kind:)":{"url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/linkersetentry(kind:)","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/LinkerSetEntry(kind:)","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Declares a record of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"kind"},{"type":"text","text":" whose value is this declaration’s."}],"kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"macro","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"LinkerSetEntry","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"(","kind":"text"},{"text":"kind","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"UInt32","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:s6UInt32V"},{"text":")","kind":"text"}],"title":"LinkerSetEntry(kind:)","role":"symbol"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/PlatformsAndLinking":{"url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/platformsandlinking","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Where records are found, how, and which test shows it."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/PlatformsAndLinking","kind":"article","title":"Platforms and linking","role":"article"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets":{"abstract":[{"text":"Declarations that register themselves at compile time and are found at run time across every module a program links.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets","kind":"symbol","role":"collection","title":"LinkerSets","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/FindingRecords":{"kind":"article","type":"topic","title":"Finding records","role":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/FindingRecords","abstract":[{"text":"Read every declaration of a kind, as the type you expect.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/findingrecords"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/LinkerSet":{"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"LinkerSet","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every record of one kind, across every image the program has loaded, and the values they"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"produce."}],"type":"topic","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"LinkerSet","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/LinkerSet","title":"LinkerSet","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/linkerset"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/WhatTheMacrosRefuse":{"type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every declaration a record can’t describe is a compile error, with a message that says why."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/WhatTheMacrosRefuse","kind":"article","role":"article","title":"What the macros refuse","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/whatthemacrosrefuse"}}}